From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Lebedev <sergey@lebedev.mobi>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 00:25:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] mtd: nand: mtk: EN751221 -- wait for the PIO write burst to
 drain, and stop reporting zero free OOB bytes

Two independent fixes, both gating a persistent writable overlay on this
board.

1. PIO page writes lost trailing bytes.

The PIO conversion of mtk_nfc_do_write_page() pushes every byte and then
immediately clears NFI_CON, tearing down CON_BWR, before PAGEPROG. The
only per-byte gate, mtk_nfc_wait_ioready(), polls NFI_PIO_DIRDY, which
reports FIFO *input* space for the next byte, not that the FIFO has been
serialised out to the NAND page register -- whatever is still queued at
teardown is dropped silently. nandtest reads back a handful of bytes per
page as 0xff, always at the end of the burst, count and victim pages
varying run-to-run -- not a fixed physical defect.

The raw-PIO read-path conversion this write path mirrors dropped
mainline's ADDRCNTR completion poll on the grounds that it's a DMA-only
counter here -- true for reads (where it wedges at 1), but never tested
for writes, where the counter is advanced by the PIO stores themselves.
NFI_FIFOSTA's WR_REMAIN field is 5 bits, so a partially-drained FIFO is
at most 31 bytes -- every observed drop is inside that.

Confirmed against the vendor's own kernel (mt6573_nand_exec_write_page):
push bytes -> poll ADDRCNTR_CNTR >= len>>9 -> clear CON_BWR -> PAGEPROG
-> wait not-busy. The middle step is what the PIO conversion dropped;
restored here, in the vendor's order.

2. jffs2 was refused outright: mtd->oobavail == 0.

Every boot logged "jffs2: inconsistent device description", emitted by
jffs2_nand_flash_setup() for exactly one reason: a NAND device
advertising zero free OOB bytes. This driver sets fdm->ecc_size =
fdm->reg_size (both 8) to match what the vendor/bootloader wrote, and
mtk_nfc_ooblayout_free() derives the free region as reg_size - ecc_size
=> 0. fdm->ecc_size still feeds NFI_PAGEFMT's FDM_ECC field and must
keep its value; only the free-region calculation is wrong to derive from
it. Reserve FDM byte 0 per sector (the BBM lane) and expose the other 7
-- 28 bytes, above jffs2's 12-byte cleanmarker.

Note the OOB read path on this driver has not been shown to round-trip:
reading OOB back returns a constant 32 bytes in the ECC region regardless
of partition, including erased ones. Test that before trusting jffs2's
OOB cleanmarkers; if it fails, UBI is the fallback since it keeps its
headers in the main page area.

Signed-off-by: Sergey Lebedev <sergey@lebedev.mobi>
---
--- a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/mtk_nand.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/mtk_nand.c
@@ -73,6 +73,9 @@
 #define		STA_EMP_PAGE		BIT(12)
 #define		NFI_FSM_CUSTDATA	(0xe << 16)
 #define		NFI_FSM_MASK		(0xf << 16)
+#define NFI_FIFOSTA		(0x64)	/* old NFI, per the vendor's mt6573v2 register header */
+#define		FIFO_WR_REMAIN(x)	(((x) & GENMASK(12, 8)) >> 8)
+#define		FIFO_WR_EMPTY		BIT(14)
 #define NFI_ADDRCNTR		(0x70)
 #define		CNTR_MASK		GENMASK(16, 12)
 #define		ADDRCNTR_SEC_SHIFT	(12)
@@ -877,8 +880,12 @@ static int mtk_nfc_do_write_page(struct
 	 * + wait for the AHB-done IRQ) times out on every page ("program ahb
 	 * done timeout", confirmed on real hardware). PIO instead, mirroring
 	 * the already-proven raw PIO read path: trigger CON_BWR then push
-	 * every byte through the DATAW port. Pushing all bytes IS the
-	 * completion, same as the read side.
+	 * every byte through the DATAW port.
+	 *
+	 * Unlike the read side, pushing all the bytes is NOT the completion:
+	 * there the CPU is the consumer, so draining the FIFO really does mean
+	 * the transfer is done, but here the CPU is the producer and the NAND
+	 * is the consumer. See the ADDRCNTR poll below.
 	 */
 	reg = nfi_readw(nfc, NFI_CNFG);
 	reg = (reg & ~CNFG_OP_MODE_MASK) | CNFG_OP_PRGM | CNFG_BYTE_RW;
@@ -897,6 +904,40 @@ static int mtk_nfc_do_write_page(struct
 		mtk_nfc_pio_write_byte(nfc, buf[i]);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Wait for the NFI to serialise the write FIFO out to the NAND page
+	 * register BEFORE tearing the burst down. mtk_nfc_wait_ioready() polls
+	 * PIO_DIRDY, which only reports FIFO *input* space for the next byte --
+	 * it says nothing about how much is still queued. Clearing CON_BWR with
+	 * bytes in flight drops them silently, which is exactly the trailing-
+	 * byte corruption seen on real hardware (a handful of bytes per page
+	 * reading back 0xff, count and victim pages varying run-to-run;
+	 * FIFO_WR_REMAIN is 5 bits, so a partial drain is <= 31 bytes, matching
+	 * the observed 2-7).
+	 *
+	 * The vendor (mt6573_nand_exec_write_page(), disassembled from the
+	 * vendor kernel) polls ADDRCNTR's sector counter and only then clears
+	 * CON_BWR, after the same push-bytes step this PIO path already does.
+	 * ADDRCNTR is DMA-only on this SoC's READ path (it wedges at 1 there,
+	 * which is why the read conversion must not poll it), but the write
+	 * path is driven by the PIO stores themselves and it does advance.
+	 */
+	if (!ret) {
+		int poll;
+
+		poll = readl_poll_timeout_atomic(nfc->regs + NFI_ADDRCNTR, reg,
+						 ADDRCNTR_SEC(reg) >=
+							chip->ecc.steps,
+						 10, MTK_TIMEOUT);
+
+		if (poll) {
+			dev_err(dev,
+				"pio write completion timeout (ADDRCNTR=%08x)\n",
+				reg);
+			ret = poll;
+		}
+	}
+
 	nfi_writel(nfc, 0, NFI_CON);
 
 	return ret;
@@ -1130,8 +1171,24 @@ static int mtk_nfc_ooblayout_free(struct
 	if (section >= eccsteps)
 		return -ERANGE;
 
-	oob_region->length = fdm->reg_size - fdm->ecc_size;
-	oob_region->offset = section * fdm->reg_size + fdm->ecc_size;
+	/*
+	 * EN751221: fdm->ecc_size is reg_size here (the vendor ECC-covers all 8
+	 * FDM bytes, and NFI_PAGEFMT's FDM_ECC field plus ecc_cfg.len are
+	 * derived from it, so it must stay that way to match bootloader-written
+	 * partitions). Deriving the *free* region from it too would leave zero
+	 * free OOB bytes -- and jffs2 refuses any NAND device with
+	 * mtd->oobavail == 0 outright ("inconsistent device description",
+	 * fs/jffs2/wbuf.c), which is what kept mount_root falling back to a
+	 * tmpfs overlay. Confirmed on hardware: /sys/class/mtd/mtd4/oobavail
+	 * read 0 with oobsize 64.
+	 *
+	 * Decouple the two: reserve FDM byte 0 of each sector (the bad-block
+	 * marker lane, same as mainline's ecc_size = 1 layout) and expose the
+	 * remaining 7 per sector -- 28 bytes total, comfortably above jffs2's
+	 * 12-byte cleanmarker. Nothing here feeds the ECC geometry.
+	 */
+	oob_region->length = fdm->reg_size - 1;
+	oob_region->offset = section * fdm->reg_size + 1;
 
 	return 0;
 }
